"Executive summary\n- End-to-end ICMP connectivity between remote-worker (10.1.0.2) and server (10.0.1.2) is completely down in both directions.\n- The branch-router is healthy at L1/L2, has correct addressing and routing, and is not filtering traffic.\n- The first L3 hop beyond the branch-router (branch-fw at 10.0.0.2) is the most likely fault domain. Evidence points to a firewall policy/NAT/routing issue or server host firewall blocking ICMP.\n\nKey observations from the insights\n- No filtering on branch-router:\n  - \u201cshow run | include ip access-group\u201d \u2192 no result (no ACLs applied to interfaces).\n  - \u201cshow ip access-lists\u201d \u2192 no result (no ACLs configured).\n- Interfaces and L2 adjacencies are good:\n  - Gi0/2 (10.1.0.1/30 to remote-worker): up/up, clean counters, ARP for 10.1.0.2 present.\n  - Gi0/1 (10.0.0.1/30 to branch-fw): up/up, clean counters, ARP for 10.0.0.2 present.\n- Correct routing on branch-router:\n  - Connected route for 10.1.0.0/30 (remote-worker).\n  - OSPF-learned route to 10.0.1.0/24 via 10.0.0.2 on Gi0/1; route stable.\n- Path tests indicate failure toward/beyond firewall:\n  - Ping from branch-router (src 10.0.0.1) to server 10.0.1.2 fails 0/5.\n  - Traceroute to 10.0.1.2 returns no replies, not even from first hop (expected 10.0.0.2). Many firewalls do not generate TTL-exceeded, so this is not conclusive alone but supports a firewall data-plane issue.\n- Traffic levels low on Gi0/1/Gi0/2 (consistent with drops/blocks), but no errors/drops on the router.\n\nMost likely fault domain\n- Branch firewall (branch-fw) or the server host itself. The router is forwarding correctly and is not blocking.\n\nPotential issues identified and how they map to symptoms\n1) Firewall security policy blocking traffic between the subnets (likely including ICMP)\n   - Why it fits: Router has routes and L2 adjacency, but cannot ping the server through the firewall. End-to-end loss in both directions is consistent with an inter-zone deny. Traceroute no first hop also aligns with firewalls dropping TTL-exceeded/ICMP.\n   - Impact: All or ICMP-only traffic between 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24 may be blocked.\n   - What to check:\n     - Inter-zone/ACL rules permitting 10.1.0.2\u219410.0.1.2 (and broader subnet pairs).\n     - If ICMP is explicitly denied; some policies allow TCP/UDP but drop ICMP.\n     - Any global policy or inspection that drops or rate-limits ICMP.\n\n2) Firewall NAT misconfiguration (missing identity/no-NAT or unintended translation between inside segments)\n   - Why it fits: If NAT is applied between internal networks, packets can be translated to unexpected addresses, leading to policy/routing mismatches and drops. Would break both directions depending on rules.\n   - What to check:\n     - NAT policies for traffic between 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24. Ensure there\u2019s exempt/identity NAT (no NAT) for internal-to-internal flows.\n     - Order of NAT rules and hit-counters to confirm which rule matches.\n     - Use packet simulation tools (e.g., ASA packet-tracer) for an ICMP echo from 10.1.0.2 to 10.0.1.2 and vice versa.\n\n3) Missing or incorrect return route on firewall/server side to 10.1.0.0/30\n   - Why it fits: Forward path exists from router to firewall for 10.0.1.0/24, but if the server side (or firewall) lacks a route back to 10.1.0.0/30 via 10.0.0.1, replies won\u2019t return. This causes 100% ICMP loss in both directions in a stateful device.\n   - What to check:\n     - Firewall routing table/OSPF on branch-fw: presence of 10.1.0.0/30 learned/installed; OSPF adjacency and LSA scope for 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24.\n     - Upstream/server-side gateway routes: ensure 10.1.0.0/30 points to branch-fw.\n     - RPF (reverse-path) or antispoofing policies that would drop traffic with \u201cunknown\u201d return route.\n\n4) Server host firewall blocking ICMP\n   - Why it fits: Router\u2192server ping fails. If the server\u2019s host firewall drops inbound ICMP echo, you would see exactly that. The alarm shows both directions fail; remote-worker could also be blocking inbound ICMP. This would create apparent end-to-end ICMP loss while other protocols may still work.\n   - What to check:\n     - On server 10.0.1.2, verify OS firewall rules allow ICMP echo/echo-reply.\n     - Test server ping to 10.0.0.1 (router). If that fails while routing/ARP is fine, host firewall is likely blocking.\n     - Similarly check remote-worker 10.1.0.2 host firewall.\n\nLower-likelihood considerations\n- Asymmetric routing: Would cause stateful firewall drops or RPF fails. Given the simple topology and OSPF route via branch-fw, less likely but confirm there isn\u2019t an alternate path.\n- Transient link reset: Single interface reset noted on router interfaces with no errors/drops and healthy status; unlikely to be root cause.\n\nRecommended next steps and remediation actions\n1) Validate basic next-hop reachability across the firewall\n   - From branch-router: ping 10.0.0.2 (branch-fw outside). If this fails, focus on L3 adjacency toward firewall.\n   - From branch-fw: ping 10.0.0.1, 10.1.0.2, and 10.0.1.2 to localize the break.\n\n2) Audit firewall security policy\n   - List and review policies between the zones that map 10.1.0.0/30 \u2194 10.0.1.0/24.\n   - Ensure rules allow at least ICMP echo/echo-reply for troubleshooting; typically you will eventually want to allow the required application protocols, not just ICMP.\n   - Check rule hit-counters/logs during test pings to confirm matches/denies.\n   - If on ASA/FTD/NGFW, run a packet simulation:\n     - Example: packet-tracer input outside icmp 10.1.0.2 8 0 10.0.1.2\n     - And the reverse path from the server zone to remote-worker.\n\n3) Verify NAT behavior on the firewall\n   - Ensure there is an identity/no-NAT rule for traffic between 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24.\n   - Confirm the NAT rule order and that no broader NAT is catching this traffic.\n   - Check xlate/connection tables for evidence of translation or drops.\n\n4) Confirm routing on firewall and server-side network\n   - On branch-fw: show ip route for 10.1.0.0/30 and 10.0.1.0/24; confirm OSPF neighbors and LSAs include both networks.\n   - On the server-side gateway (if not the firewall): ensure a route to 10.1.0.0/30 via the firewall exists.\n   - Check for uRPF/antispoofing that could drop return traffic.\n\n5) Check host firewalls\n   - On server 10.0.1.2 and remote-worker 10.1.0.2, temporarily allow ICMP to validate.\n   - If ICMP starts working, craft permanent, least-privilege exceptions or proceed to test actual application ports.\n\n6) Retest and monitor\n   - After policy/NAT/route corrections, retest ping both directions:\n     - remote-worker \u2192 server\n     - server \u2192 remote-worker\n     - router (10.0.0.1) \u2192 server (10.0.1.2)\n   - Verify router Gi0/1 traffic increases and that there are no drops.\n   - Optionally test application traffic to ensure this is not an ICMP-only issue.\n\nConclusion\n- The branch-router is correctly configured and operational; the failure domain is at or beyond the branch firewall. The most actionable areas are firewall inter-zone policy, NAT exemptions for internal-to-internal traffic, and correct return routing. Host-based ICMP blocking on the server and/or remote-worker could also contribute. Address these in order to restore end-to-end reachability."